    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured `OWNER_CREATED` audit event with the lowest structural impact: a single-file change to `SaveOwner`, the class that already owns the `AUDIT` logger and already computes the owner id, `customerCode`, and `membershipLevel`.\n\n**`src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/SaveOwner.java`** \u2014 added, alongside the existing audit line:\n- A static `AtomicLong EVENT_SEQ` for a monotonically increasing sequence across creates.\n- A one-line `primaryId` local capturing the owner's *current* primary identifier (`customerCode`), so the later memberId unification is a single-line change here \u2014 exactly as the spec's forward-looking note requires.\n- A second `AUDIT` emission of the immutable JSON event `{\"seq\",...,\"ownerId\",...,\"customerCode\",...,\"membershipLevel\",...,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`.\n\nSample emitted event:\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\n**Why this keeps structural cost low:** `SaveOwner` has a single method (`WMC_other = 0 \u2192 1`), the method has cyclomatic complexity 1 (no branches added), and only one file is touched \u2014 so the multiplicative file factor stays at 1. I deliberately inlined the JSON rather than adding a new function class + YAML step, which would have doubled the files-touched multiplier for no behavioural gain.\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest.coreEventCarriesIdAndIdentifier` passes, and the full `./mvnw test` run is `BUILD SUCCESS` (1 test, 0 failures).",
